<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carlos Manuel de Céspedes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a>–<a href="/years/1874">1874</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carlos Manuel de Céspedes was a Cuban planter and lawyer who freed his enslaved workers and proclaimed Cuban independence at his estate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-demajagua">La Demajagua</a> on 10 October 1868, starting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-years-war">Ten Years' War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In 1869 the assembly of Guáimaro elected him first President of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in Arms. Deposed in 1873, he was killed by Spanish troops in 1874.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carlos-manuel-de-cespedes.jpg" alt="Carlos Manuel de Céspedes" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elfisie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carlos_Manuel_de_Cespedes_y_del_Castillo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In August 1933 an army revolt joined a general strike and sugar workers' uprisings to drive Machado out, and in September the Sergeants' Revolt, led by Sergeant Fulgencio Batista, overthrew <b>Carlos Manuel de Céspedes</b> y Quesada and the Pentarchy that followed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The first &quot;La Bayamesa&quot; was composed in 1851 by <b>Carlos Manuel de Céspedes</b> and José Fornaris at the request of their friend Francisco Castillo Moreno, who is sometimes also credited with its words; after 1868, during the Cuban war, a &quot;mambí&quot; version with the same melody and different…</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-bayamesa">La Bayamesa</a></li><li><blockquote>On 10 October 1868 the planter <b>Carlos Manuel de Céspedes</b> freed his enslaved workers and proclaimed independence at La Demajagua, starting the Ten Years' War.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-cuba-in-arms">Republic of Cuba in Arms</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bayamo, in what is now <a href="/countries/cuba">Cuba</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-maestra">Sierra Maestra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cuba">Cuba</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.38,-76.64&amp;year=1854" aria-label="Bayamo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2485 1409 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Carlos Manuel de Céspedes"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2935 1679h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2935 1679h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.38,-76.64&amp;year=1854">What was here in 1854? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
